1
00:00:13,585 --> 00:00:17,827
[SPEAKER_00]: How would you define the comfort zone, in your own words?

2
00:00:17,847 --> 00:00:41,038
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, to me, your comfort zone is that your routine, your way of being, which distracts you, which distracts you from your discomfort, from your

3
00:00:42,667 --> 00:00:43,887
[SPEAKER_01]: Fundamental discomfort.

4
00:00:45,068 --> 00:00:47,488
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's that which keeps a lid on your unhappiness.

5
00:00:48,848 --> 00:00:49,989
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, right.

6
00:00:50,989 --> 00:00:57,970
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it helps you... it helps distract you and shelters you from dissatisfaction.

7
00:00:58,030 --> 00:01:00,851
[SPEAKER_00]: But what dissatisfaction?

8
00:01:00,871 --> 00:01:01,271
[SPEAKER_00]: Of what?

9
00:01:01,371 --> 00:01:01,771
[SPEAKER_00]: With what?

10
00:01:05,512 --> 00:01:08,173
[SPEAKER_01]: So, not necessarily pain.

11
00:01:10,311 --> 00:01:13,253
[SPEAKER_01]: Of things not going the way you want them to go.

12
00:01:14,174 --> 00:01:17,677
[SPEAKER_00]: So, dissatisfaction of not being in control, fundamentally.

13
00:01:18,217 --> 00:01:27,924
[SPEAKER_00]: The reason why I'm trying to clarify this is because it's not... it's a subtle... it goes on a very subtle level.

14
00:01:27,944 --> 00:01:32,868
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not just a dissatisfaction on like a kind that people can easily relate to.

15
00:01:33,329 --> 00:01:37,532
[SPEAKER_00]: It's something you will need to dig in and dig in and do lots of your cover-ups.

16
00:01:38,907 --> 00:01:56,781
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, discover that you depend on comfort and comfort zone, because, basically, it provides you... well, it helps you entertain the notion that you are in control, that you do have a say, that you are your own, and that, you know, basically, you will be.

17
00:01:56,801 --> 00:01:59,503
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's a sense of that existential safety that comes from it.

18
00:02:00,764 --> 00:02:01,405
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you are...

19
00:02:04,120 --> 00:02:06,622
[SPEAKER_01]: You're dissatisfied because you're not in control.

20
00:02:07,482 --> 00:02:10,264
[SPEAKER_01]: Fundamentally, you... Well, exactly.

21
00:02:10,344 --> 00:02:17,188
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're dissatisfied because you're recognizing that even your control is not up to you.

22
00:02:17,528 --> 00:02:19,449
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not within your say, fundamentally.

23
00:02:19,829 --> 00:02:23,111
[SPEAKER_00]: Even things you can control, it's only relatively so.

24
00:02:23,151 --> 00:02:30,055
[SPEAKER_00]: And a relative control, it's not really the control that you need to reach the level of safety.

25
00:02:30,075 --> 00:02:32,136
[SPEAKER_00]: You need the absolute control, at least implicitly.

26
00:02:32,992 --> 00:02:33,994
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to be your own.

27
00:02:34,775 --> 00:02:40,004
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, that's... that's like, you know, the... and always that sense of, like, not seeing a nature results in possessions.

28
00:02:41,066 --> 00:02:43,209
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Buddha would say, it results in acquisitions.

29
00:02:44,552 --> 00:02:46,134
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because you acquired things.

30
00:02:47,823 --> 00:02:48,944
[SPEAKER_00]: Physically, necessarily.

31
00:02:49,525 --> 00:02:59,895
[SPEAKER_00]: But because, internally, you refuse to admit and acknowledge and discern the inherent anicca of these things, that they are not controllable, thus not ownable.

32
00:03:00,996 --> 00:03:10,326
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if you have no... if you renounce all the worldly possessions, but internally, you refuse to face the anicca that fills you with anxiety, you are taking all of the possessions of the world.

33
00:03:11,203 --> 00:03:12,604
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you still have the self-view.

34
00:03:13,044 --> 00:03:16,347
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your only way of dealing with it.

35
00:03:16,467 --> 00:03:17,468
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

36
00:03:17,488 --> 00:03:21,791
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't know how to deal with that frightening recognition that you are not your own.

37
00:03:22,712 --> 00:03:23,452
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what do I do?

38
00:03:23,492 --> 00:03:24,093
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll make every...

39
00:03:24,593 --> 00:03:25,514
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll make things my own.

40
00:03:25,934 --> 00:03:26,594
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, exactly.

41
00:03:26,735 --> 00:03:30,898
[SPEAKER_00]: Or at least, I'll find things that will enable me to pretend that things are my own.

42
00:03:31,398 --> 00:03:37,603
[SPEAKER_00]: And then just wishfully hope that things will not go against the way I wanted them to go.

43
00:03:38,430 --> 00:03:42,453
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if they do, I will not be able to deal with it, and I will be overwhelmed by suffering, and suffer, and so on.

44
00:03:43,134 --> 00:03:45,456
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why people try to outrun anxiety, and the whole thing.

45
00:03:46,116 --> 00:03:54,023
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why in the comfort zone... And some people, you know, need more of a comfort... have a bigger comfort zone, so people have a lesser comfort zone, so to speak.

46
00:03:54,263 --> 00:03:54,984
[SPEAKER_01]: Or dependency.

47
00:03:55,444 --> 00:03:56,425
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean.

48
00:03:56,725 --> 00:04:01,028
[SPEAKER_00]: The size of the comfort zone determines your dependency on the comfort zone.

49
00:04:01,349 --> 00:04:07,053
[SPEAKER_00]: But either way, if you start looking at it, it can be a good symptom to see how much of a comfort zone you need for yourself.

50
00:04:07,373 --> 00:04:12,837
[SPEAKER_00]: It can be a good kind of indicator of how much of a cover-up you're engaging with on that existential level.

51
00:04:14,378 --> 00:04:16,160
[SPEAKER_00]: And even in mundane worldly terms,

52
00:04:16,936 --> 00:04:27,822
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, people who are afraid to even think about the extent of their comfort zone, and just want to commit to it, and not question it, and not have the slightest doubt about it, are usually people that don't get anywhere in their life.

53
00:04:29,703 --> 00:04:37,927
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, like, even just a worldly ambition requires taking some risk, requires recognizing degree that you... a certain degree of non-control.

54
00:04:38,547 --> 00:04:39,568
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you take the risk.

55
00:04:39,708 --> 00:04:40,548
[SPEAKER_00]: Things can go either way.

56
00:04:40,868 --> 00:04:43,770
[SPEAKER_00]: But... and because of that, you kind of better yourself.

57
00:04:45,140 --> 00:04:49,946
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even in worldly terms, stepping outside of the comfort zone is actually beneficial.

58
00:04:51,489 --> 00:04:57,677
[SPEAKER_01]: But as soon as you step out of that comfort zone, for many people, there's anxiety of what might happen.

59
00:04:57,697 --> 00:04:58,318
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

60
00:04:58,478 --> 00:05:02,123
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where you force, then, your mind to grow.

61
00:05:03,149 --> 00:05:06,810
[SPEAKER_00]: If there is no stress, there will be no response of growth.

62
00:05:07,070 --> 00:05:08,251
[SPEAKER_00]: Same, physically, mentally.

63
00:05:08,891 --> 00:05:16,773
[SPEAKER_00]: If there is no tightening of the pressure in that sense, there is no reason for body and mind to adapt to the new pressuring environment.

64
00:05:16,833 --> 00:05:17,574
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it won't grow.

65
00:05:17,694 --> 00:05:18,914
[SPEAKER_01]: It won't become stronger.

66
00:05:19,034 --> 00:05:24,796
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, another person doubles down on not... Doubles down on sheltering himself from anything.

67
00:05:24,996 --> 00:05:28,217
[SPEAKER_00]: Which results then in doubling down on the weakness, increasing the weakness.

68
00:05:28,637 --> 00:05:30,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Indirectly, but that's the outcome of it.

69
00:05:31,459 --> 00:05:42,784
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the more you refuse to accept these things on a mental level, that you're not in control, that anxiety is a natural, basically, part of the existence, eh... the weaker, the more sensitive to it you will become.

70
00:05:45,846 --> 00:05:52,049
[SPEAKER_00]: And the more you try to get rid of it, the more power you give it.

71
00:05:53,970 --> 00:05:58,572
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, how far would you then want to go, in terms of

72
00:06:00,099 --> 00:06:10,426
[SPEAKER_00]: So, speaking from the point of view of the practice, say, how far would you want to go in terms of questioning or sort of disturbing your own comfort zone?

73
00:06:12,788 --> 00:06:15,830
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, would you just, like, go and risk your life?

74
00:06:15,850 --> 00:06:21,253
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, fundamentally, you know, being alive is a form of a comfort zone.

75
00:06:21,313 --> 00:06:23,735
[SPEAKER_00]: What criteria would you use as to how far to go?

76
00:06:25,676 --> 00:06:26,477
[SPEAKER_00]: Give everything up,

77
00:06:28,193 --> 00:06:30,615
[SPEAKER_00]: Quit your job, everything, everything, and go and live on the street.

78
00:06:31,796 --> 00:06:34,138
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, you know, I don't want to depend on the comfort zone.

79
00:06:34,158 --> 00:06:35,599
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how far should a person go?

80
00:06:36,960 --> 00:06:40,343
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, why are they going?

81
00:06:40,363 --> 00:06:41,504
[SPEAKER_01]: Why are they stepping out?

82
00:06:42,084 --> 00:06:43,566
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they want to practice.

83
00:06:43,986 --> 00:06:44,206
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

84
00:06:44,226 --> 00:06:45,947
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're stepping out is...

85
00:06:45,967 --> 00:06:48,710
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how far do they need to step out?

86
00:06:48,730 --> 00:06:57,477
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, if somebody, say, a layperson wants to practice, maybe in foraranshi, do they just get up, walk out,

87
00:06:58,740 --> 00:07:06,733
[SPEAKER_00]: Not say a word and leave everything behind and just go and live under a bridge or somewhere.

88
00:07:07,174 --> 00:07:08,476
[SPEAKER_00]: How far do they have to go?

89
00:07:09,779 --> 00:07:18,045
[SPEAKER_01]: They would have to give up things which are unwholesome, obviously, but... Sure.

90
00:07:18,986 --> 00:07:27,192
[SPEAKER_00]: But, you know, not eating in the afternoon and being a celibate is, yeah, requires you to give up things that are unwholesome.

91
00:07:27,712 --> 00:07:33,797
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, like walking out and living under a bridge also requires you to not eat in the afternoon, because you won't have any food to eat.

92
00:07:35,187 --> 00:07:39,588
[SPEAKER_00]: So, either way, you'll be giving up on wholesome, but there are obviously two different ways of going about it.

93
00:07:39,688 --> 00:07:46,710
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what would... what criteria could a person use, then, to know how far is, like, too far?

94
00:07:47,510 --> 00:07:48,810
[SPEAKER_01]: Outside of my comfort zone.

95
00:07:48,870 --> 00:07:54,732
[SPEAKER_00]: Testing my comfort zone, uprooting my comfort zone, and how far is just now being ridiculous and risky?

96
00:07:56,152 --> 00:08:00,313
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it would be... it's... it's a very... it's actually a common... you could use a common sense thing.

97
00:08:01,477 --> 00:08:10,206
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Buddha did in the suttas, and I think it's in the Sābhāsa Sutta as well, certain things to be abandoned by avoiding.

98
00:08:11,267 --> 00:08:17,933
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you recognize that... First, you kind of recognize the extent of your comfort zone.

99
00:08:18,093 --> 00:08:18,654
[SPEAKER_00]: And I don't mean...

100
00:08:18,814 --> 00:08:22,417
[SPEAKER_00]: The comfort zone is not always simply defined by comfort, physical comfort.

101
00:08:22,838 --> 00:08:24,379
[SPEAKER_00]: More often than not, it's the mental

102
00:08:25,258 --> 00:08:28,581
[SPEAKER_00]: Comfort and need and habit and access to things.

103
00:08:28,601 --> 00:08:30,864
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what people kind of are resting on.

104
00:08:31,224 --> 00:08:32,225
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to question that.

105
00:08:32,665 --> 00:08:37,350
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the most immediate way of questioning and disturbing your comfort zone is sense restraint.

106
00:08:38,771 --> 00:08:41,774
[SPEAKER_00]: That will already gonna fill plenty of people with anxiety and panic.

107
00:08:42,602 --> 00:08:43,983
[SPEAKER_00]: Just saying no to their desires.

108
00:08:44,803 --> 00:08:49,146
[SPEAKER_00]: And you realize, most people wouldn't even need more than that, in a way.

109
00:08:49,806 --> 00:09:03,415
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, if afterwards, that... they get used to that, sense restraint, celibacy, and then they feel like they're stalling and not really making any significant progress, then they could, you know...

110
00:09:04,055 --> 00:09:05,476
[SPEAKER_00]: Start digging things a bit further.

111
00:09:05,616 --> 00:09:06,956
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Buddha said, delve with a knife.

112
00:09:07,596 --> 00:09:11,378
[SPEAKER_00]: Just keep cutting and cutting until there's no more work for you to be done.

113
00:09:11,578 --> 00:09:12,898
[SPEAKER_00]: At the same time, you use the common sense.

114
00:09:13,018 --> 00:09:17,760
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you are not enlightened, you are not free from suffering, you don't know the escape.

115
00:09:18,820 --> 00:09:31,605
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, you need to uproot your comfort zone and your dependence upon it, but you don't want to do it to the extent whereby you will factually be risking your life, because you might die unenlightened and not knowing anything.

116
00:09:33,072 --> 00:09:48,120
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's... because there were those practices even back in the day, and I'm sure some people would still have those views today, of like, oh, I'll just say yes to everything, or I say no to everything, or I just walk straight, that was like in the suttas, and doesn't matter what, you never... you don't go left, you don't go right, you just keep walking straight.

117
00:09:48,580 --> 00:09:52,542
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if there is a cliff, you'll just walk through, because that's the strength of your determination.

118
00:09:53,182 --> 00:09:57,164
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how much you will not depend on any comfort or reason for that matter.

119
00:09:57,825 --> 00:09:59,365
[SPEAKER_00]: But the Buddha would say, no, certain things are to

120
00:10:02,622 --> 00:10:10,509
[SPEAKER_00]: Wisely, you avoid a raging elephant that's about to stomp you, a rabid dog, a group of bandits that might kill you.

121
00:10:11,629 --> 00:10:11,870
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

122
00:10:11,950 --> 00:10:17,094
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if they do, you will lose your life without having achieved liberation and so on.

123
00:10:17,594 --> 00:10:22,799
[SPEAKER_00]: After you achieve liberation, if you choose to stand in front of an elephant, that's fine, because you have nothing to lose anymore.

124
00:10:23,499 --> 00:10:25,421
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you might not worry about it.

125
00:10:26,141 --> 00:10:28,023
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't want to lose perspective

126
00:10:29,903 --> 00:10:35,225
[SPEAKER_00]: On your goal, which is to be free from suffering, enlightened, free from saṃsāra and so on.

127
00:10:35,385 --> 00:10:38,226
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you want to question your comfort zone, you question it in those manners.

128
00:10:38,246 --> 00:10:53,092
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how can I test or challenge my comfort zone without risking my life or compromising my health significantly, that that would occupy all my time and I wouldn't be able to practice and so on.

129
00:10:54,600 --> 00:11:05,891
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you would even, like as I said, it would be obvious, there's nothing harmful and there's nothing stopping you in practicing sensory strength, even within your current comfort zone.

130
00:11:06,392 --> 00:11:09,015
[SPEAKER_00]: So you don't need to, I must go and live on the street and so on.

131
00:11:09,675 --> 00:11:11,317
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I just need to be celibate.

132
00:11:12,520 --> 00:11:13,020
[SPEAKER_00]: And there you go.

133
00:11:13,601 --> 00:11:16,983
[SPEAKER_00]: Your entire world of comfort is uprooted by that determination and decision.

134
00:11:17,943 --> 00:11:21,966
[SPEAKER_00]: And you would feel it, even without necessarily having to change the circumstances significantly.

135
00:11:22,506 --> 00:11:32,593
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, sure, if significant... if the circumstances are too testing, too challenging for you to practice sense restraint, then, yeah, you want to find somewhere else where you can.

136
00:11:33,597 --> 00:11:34,598
[SPEAKER_00]: But all from the...

137
00:11:34,658 --> 00:11:41,202
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the reference point should be the practice, the liberation and the achieving the goal.

138
00:11:41,662 --> 00:11:47,465
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like some sort of ideal of I must live on the street independent of others and never eat for the rest of my life.

139
00:11:47,485 --> 00:11:47,606
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

140
00:11:47,626 --> 00:11:50,587
[SPEAKER_00]: There's just another thing now that you're trying to replace your comfort zone with.

141
00:11:50,928 --> 00:11:51,548
[SPEAKER_01]: Asceticism.

142
00:11:51,728 --> 00:11:52,128
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

143
00:11:52,248 --> 00:11:52,869
[SPEAKER_01]: Asceticism.

144
00:11:52,889 --> 00:11:56,191
[SPEAKER_00]: This extreme... Where you just shun all the comfort.

145
00:11:56,758 --> 00:11:58,840
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, yeah, but then you become dependent on that.

146
00:11:59,681 --> 00:12:05,367
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to get rid of that dependency on something.

147
00:12:05,528 --> 00:12:05,908
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

148
00:12:06,068 --> 00:12:07,029
[SPEAKER_00]: But not replace it.

149
00:12:08,270 --> 00:12:14,637
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's the case when, you know, in the... when I said the ascetic practices are ignoble.

150
00:12:15,587 --> 00:12:17,768
[SPEAKER_00]: And don't result in liberation.

151
00:12:17,808 --> 00:12:20,548
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because you don't really uproot any dependency.

152
00:12:21,008 --> 00:12:28,170
[SPEAKER_00]: You just shun all the comfort and everything, and that becomes your thing you depend on.

153
00:12:29,430 --> 00:12:30,431
[SPEAKER_00]: Constant saying no.

154
00:12:31,631 --> 00:12:36,312
[SPEAKER_00]: And either way, you haven't really uprooted that existential dependence, which requires you to understand it.

155
00:12:36,752 --> 00:12:37,412
[SPEAKER_00]: Understand this thing.

156
00:12:37,432 --> 00:12:40,353
[SPEAKER_00]: In which case, if you do, you might not need to.

157
00:12:42,112 --> 00:12:45,791
[SPEAKER_00]: Change things too significantly, if they were already conducive for wholesomeness.

158
00:12:48,677 --> 00:12:53,781
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean, like, it's not that you must give everything and live under the root of a tree.

159
00:12:54,361 --> 00:12:59,565
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, if you see that directly conducive to those wholesome mental states, then do it.

160
00:12:59,605 --> 00:13:03,848
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's when the Buddha would say, let those who want to do that, do that, the ascetic practices.

161
00:13:03,968 --> 00:13:11,614
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, meaning, let those who know that these ascetic practices will bring wholesome things to increase, unwholesome things to decrease, let them do it.

162
00:13:12,294 --> 00:13:13,295
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not always the case.

163
00:13:13,335 --> 00:13:20,001
[SPEAKER_00]: For some people, shunning too much comfort too soon will actually bring the unwholesome to increase and wholesome to decrease.

164
00:13:21,883 --> 00:13:33,855
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the reference point should be the practice and liberation and freedom from suffering, not necessarily a sense of duty and what you kind of romantically assume you must be living with and giving up.

165
00:13:35,409 --> 00:13:50,028
[SPEAKER_00]: So, to be free from suffering, to be free from that dissatisfaction... To be free from that, yeah, that, basically, from the dissatisfaction that arises on account of fundamental existential non-control.

166
00:13:51,735 --> 00:13:53,317
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's your goal, that's your criteria.

167
00:13:54,178 --> 00:13:57,942
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't want to be depend... and that's... that's like... you don't want to be depending on anything.

168
00:13:58,443 --> 00:14:02,268
[SPEAKER_00]: It's when that's... the Buddha would say, you don't find anything as a support.

169
00:14:02,608 --> 00:14:04,610
[SPEAKER_00]: Your consciousness is not supported by anything.

170
00:14:05,397 --> 00:14:07,959
[SPEAKER_00]: any other aggregate and so on.

171
00:14:08,020 --> 00:14:16,547
[SPEAKER_00]: And the simile was that the cow, the flayed cow with the wounds, that if it leans on the wall, it's being eaten by the creatures that live on the wall.

172
00:14:16,587 --> 00:14:19,370
[SPEAKER_00]: If it lays on the ground, it's being eaten by the creatures that live there.

173
00:14:19,430 --> 00:14:22,333
[SPEAKER_00]: If it stands up, it's being eaten by the creatures that are flying there.

174
00:14:22,873 --> 00:14:27,418
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, if it tries to use anything as a support, it's being devoured.

175
00:14:28,661 --> 00:14:29,121
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's it.

176
00:14:29,221 --> 00:14:41,210
[SPEAKER_00]: If you try to use anything as your comfort zone, on account of which you don't face the reality that you are a wounded animal subjected to death, not in your control and so on, you will suffer.

177
00:14:41,610 --> 00:14:44,652
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because of the wounds, but because you're trying to cover it up.

178
00:14:47,561 --> 00:14:53,603
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the dissatisfaction comes because you don't want to acknowledge and admit that you're not in control, because it's too frightening.

179
00:14:53,623 --> 00:14:55,524
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as you said before, like pain is not the problem?

180
00:14:55,844 --> 00:14:56,504
[SPEAKER_00]: Pain is not the problem.

181
00:14:56,524 --> 00:14:59,905
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's that resistance towards it, which makes it painful.

182
00:15:00,045 --> 00:15:01,366
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what the dukkha is, yeah.

183
00:15:01,686 --> 00:15:10,289
[SPEAKER_00]: Physical discomfort is not the dukkha that you should be sort of freeing yourself from, because the body is not in your control.

184
00:15:10,329 --> 00:15:11,329
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't free yourself from it.

185
00:15:11,349 --> 00:15:14,190
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why even Arahants would say, any disturbance that's left

186
00:15:15,222 --> 00:15:20,707
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, after I become an arahant, any disturbance that's left, it's on account of this body, until it breaks apart.

187
00:15:21,367 --> 00:15:27,112
[SPEAKER_00]: So, any annoyance, disturbance, just physical, none of that will cause mental anguish or suffering.

188
00:15:29,074 --> 00:15:32,096
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to be doing things gradually to put... Yeah.

189
00:15:32,176 --> 00:15:33,998
[SPEAKER_01]: In terms of the practice, you want to be revealing

190
00:15:36,464 --> 00:15:37,044
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, exactly.

191
00:15:37,064 --> 00:15:42,967
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't want to just go through the motions of giving things up and questioning and challenging my comfort zone.

192
00:15:43,628 --> 00:15:44,828
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to have the context.

193
00:15:44,888 --> 00:15:48,590
[SPEAKER_00]: Context being, I am existentially depending upon a sense of control.

194
00:15:50,331 --> 00:15:53,453
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's basically... my comfort zone is the symptom of that.

195
00:15:54,754 --> 00:15:57,195
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I don't want to be existentially depending.

196
00:15:57,355 --> 00:15:59,937
[SPEAKER_00]: As Will would say, I don't want to be using anything as a support.

197
00:16:01,540 --> 00:16:02,040
[SPEAKER_00]: Internally.

198
00:16:03,261 --> 00:16:03,802
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

199
00:16:03,822 --> 00:16:07,424
[SPEAKER_01]: I just want everything that I use to be remind me of the nature of things.

200
00:16:07,464 --> 00:16:09,566
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, that's like... if you gain perspective, sure.

201
00:16:09,926 --> 00:16:14,289
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to say... you want to do things that keep that... that can sustain that context alive.

202
00:16:14,309 --> 00:16:23,035
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so it's... you... there are obvious things that you will have to stop doing, things that will be completely and unequivocally always distracting you from that context.

203
00:16:23,555 --> 00:16:28,499
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensuality, carelessness, breaking the precepts, taking drugs, alcohol and so on.

204
00:16:28,699 --> 00:16:38,704
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, decoration, that's why it says, asks of us to be abandoned by using the four requisites properly, you know, just for covering up the shameful parts.

205
00:16:38,724 --> 00:16:41,205
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, by rewriting the purpose of things you use them for.

206
00:16:41,925 --> 00:16:48,288
[SPEAKER_00]: Not for beautification and decoration, but for the practical, basically, sustenance and need, until you have fulfilled your goal.

207
00:16:49,590 --> 00:16:50,651
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's another thing.

208
00:16:50,971 --> 00:16:58,397
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you would know exactly what is conducive and what is not conducive, the clearer your goal is.

209
00:17:00,260 --> 00:17:05,981
[SPEAKER_00]: So, most people practice the dhamma, have a vague notion of some goal, but they don't really... they can't relate to it internally.

210
00:17:06,021 --> 00:17:10,662
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't see a direct line between, if I do this, will result in that, and...

211
00:17:10,702 --> 00:17:22,665
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all kind of over there, and wishful thinking, and hoping that it will result like the way they want it to result, but... Do you... Are you clear as to what needs to be done, in terms of the dhamma practice?

212
00:17:23,725 --> 00:17:29,847
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, as we would say, do you, like, you hear, you measure the task, and then the inspiration comes, because now you know exactly how much you need to

213
00:17:32,565 --> 00:17:36,047
[SPEAKER_00]: So, internally, your goal should be to not depend on anything.

214
00:17:36,827 --> 00:17:51,715
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, any feeling, or any perception, or any circumstance, any, basically, anything that provides you with comfort, and that sense of, sort of, like, resting upon something, like, ah, I'm safe here, you are not in any position of safety.

215
00:17:51,756 --> 00:17:56,818
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that should be the context you should only be increasing in your practice, the recognition of that context.

216
00:17:57,599 --> 00:17:59,780
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's the true way to find the safety, then.

217
00:18:01,148 --> 00:18:12,798
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, so, I step outside of my comfort zone, in the right way, so... And that might make you tougher, might make you grow a bit, but if you do it just for the sake of doing it, you are not going to get any substantial wisdom.

218
00:18:13,678 --> 00:18:19,683
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you can do it with the intention to try and learn from it and so on, but even then, then you use the common sense.

219
00:18:19,703 --> 00:18:22,305
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let me not do to the extent that I cannot sustain.

220
00:18:22,906 --> 00:18:29,731
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, I can go and live under a bridge without anything or anyone for a bit, before I get sick and basically die or something.

221
00:18:30,796 --> 00:18:39,743
[SPEAKER_00]: Or I can go and live in this tiger-infested jungle, but there's a good chance, you know, I might die within a month, because there are man-eaters there, and so on.

222
00:18:40,483 --> 00:18:41,984
[SPEAKER_00]: So, well, that's foolish then.

223
00:18:43,966 --> 00:18:50,350
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there are plenty of other non-life-threatening ways that you can challenge and uproot your confidence on.

224
00:18:50,751 --> 00:18:51,351
[SPEAKER_00]: Be celibate.

225
00:18:52,572 --> 00:18:53,453
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't eat in the afternoon.

226
00:18:54,814 --> 00:18:56,495
[SPEAKER_00]: Restrain your eyes, nose, ears.

227
00:18:57,195 --> 00:18:59,177
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't go after the pleasure of sense objects.

228
00:19:01,243 --> 00:19:13,178
[SPEAKER_00]: That will shake you right to the core, because the core of the human existence is that central being, being of centrality, being that depends upon the pleasures obtained through the object of the senses.

229
00:19:13,899 --> 00:19:16,562
[SPEAKER_00]: And with the said that, Putrajana doesn't know any other escape.

230
00:19:17,294 --> 00:19:22,058
[SPEAKER_00]: From the discomfort, from the unpleasant feeling, except sensuality.

231
00:19:22,618 --> 00:19:26,682
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why he's like double, sort of twice bound by it.

232
00:19:27,202 --> 00:19:34,128
[SPEAKER_00]: First, because he wants pleasure, but also because that's his only way of avoiding pain and discomfort.

233
00:19:35,369 --> 00:19:36,450
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the distraction.

234
00:19:37,330 --> 00:19:40,353
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only way to deal with things, to deal with the nature.

235
00:19:41,142 --> 00:19:42,524
[SPEAKER_00]: It's to turn a blind eye to it.

236
00:19:42,544 --> 00:19:43,225
[SPEAKER_00]: To distract yourself.

237
00:19:43,245 --> 00:19:43,866
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

238
00:19:44,087 --> 00:19:45,128
[SPEAKER_01]: Find things to do.

239
00:19:45,829 --> 00:19:47,932
[SPEAKER_01]: Remove, removing distractions.

240
00:19:48,954 --> 00:19:51,358
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, you will reveal the nature.

241
00:19:52,485 --> 00:19:53,165
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, exactly.

242
00:19:53,205 --> 00:20:01,527
[SPEAKER_00]: Gradually, if you refuse to engage with distractions, first, you're going to have just a good old day, plain day-to-day boredom.

243
00:20:02,427 --> 00:20:07,368
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you still don't give in and try to distract yourself, yeah, that boredom, you're going to start feeling the weight of it.

244
00:20:07,408 --> 00:20:09,368
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you realize, oh, this is something much more.

245
00:20:10,428 --> 00:20:20,110
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you start feeling the weight of it, meaning more anxiety, the anxiety that comes from seeing the nature of you not being controlled, the nature.

246
00:20:21,635 --> 00:20:36,843
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you stick with that long enough, and don't try to run away from that nature, which is... Well, you will force your mind to grow, because that's how you grow, that's how you become stronger, by by stressing it.

247
00:20:38,244 --> 00:20:43,347
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, sure, you could stress it too much, and then just have a breakdown, because you can't bear that anxiety.

248
00:20:44,636 --> 00:20:56,188
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you have sensory strain beforehand, if you kept the precepts beforehand, if you don't have the negative unwholesome outlets of your behavior, like bad habits and so on, you are actually compressing it and you're forcing it to adapt.

249
00:20:57,029 --> 00:21:05,198
[SPEAKER_00]: So, once you become more and more used to anxiety and not trying to outrun it, then you realize that the only reason it was unpleasant is you... because you kept trying to outrun it.

250
00:21:05,658 --> 00:21:06,539
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because in itself.

251
00:21:07,179 --> 00:21:13,842
[SPEAKER_00]: Sense of control... sorry, sense of non-control that people can recognize in anicca, it's quite neutral.

252
00:21:14,383 --> 00:21:16,824
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing... there's nothing wrong or unpleasant in it.

253
00:21:16,844 --> 00:21:22,007
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you don't suffer on account of... you have no control over the tree or the river.

254
00:21:22,667 --> 00:21:23,628
[SPEAKER_00]: And that doesn't bother you.

255
00:21:24,168 --> 00:21:24,508
[SPEAKER_00]: Why not?

256
00:21:25,028 --> 00:21:27,630
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's still the phenomenon of non-control that you recognize.

257
00:21:28,852 --> 00:21:31,934
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't bother you, because you're not personally invested in controlling it.

258
00:21:32,814 --> 00:21:44,299
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, you realize that when any sense of non-control bothers you, it's only that bother is actually caused by your investment of, I want to control this, or I assume to control this.

259
00:21:44,680 --> 00:21:47,281
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now, the recognition of non-control is undermining that.

260
00:21:48,021 --> 00:21:51,943
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you remove your assumption, no amount of non-control can cause you suffering.

261
00:21:52,043 --> 00:21:53,024
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just as it is.

262
00:21:54,324 --> 00:21:55,745
[SPEAKER_00]: The way things are, in that sense.

263
00:21:55,825 --> 00:21:57,546
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, things are truly anicca.

264
00:21:58,461 --> 00:22:04,132
[SPEAKER_00]: But you see it or not, the only reason anicca will be dukkha is if you ignore the fact that they are anicca.

265
00:22:04,693 --> 00:22:08,882
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's because of your ignorance that anicca is dukkha, not because in itself anicca is dukkha.

266
00:22:10,259 --> 00:22:15,460
[SPEAKER_01]: You see, you won't at any stage change that fact of anicca.

267
00:22:15,680 --> 00:22:16,441
[SPEAKER_01]: Inconceivable.

268
00:22:16,621 --> 00:22:17,461
[SPEAKER_01]: Inconceivable.

269
00:22:18,621 --> 00:22:20,642
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll practice in such a way that anicca changes.

270
00:22:20,702 --> 00:22:21,062
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

271
00:22:21,142 --> 00:22:23,622
[SPEAKER_01]: You stop going against that.

272
00:22:23,722 --> 00:22:28,403
[SPEAKER_00]: You stop entertaining assumptions that are diametrically opposed to anicca, to the nature that you recognize.

273
00:22:28,863 --> 00:22:31,544
[SPEAKER_00]: So anxiety is a good symptom, basically.

274
00:22:31,604 --> 00:22:39,786
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're bothered by anxiety, it's not because anxiety bothers you, it's because of your views that are opposed to that which anxiety is revealing, which is non-control.

275
00:22:40,666 --> 00:22:42,067
[SPEAKER_00]: So, stop entertaining control.

276
00:22:42,367 --> 00:22:46,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop entertaining the control of... through your possessions, of your possessions.

277
00:22:46,570 --> 00:22:47,770
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop entertaining ownership.

278
00:22:48,591 --> 00:22:50,512
[SPEAKER_00]: Because all you need to do is start thinking about it.

279
00:22:50,632 --> 00:22:53,374
[SPEAKER_00]: And even things that you do own, just think about it.

280
00:22:53,634 --> 00:22:56,055
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you prevent them from disappearing or something happening to them?

281
00:22:56,175 --> 00:22:56,836
[SPEAKER_00]: Inconceivable.

282
00:22:57,396 --> 00:22:59,957
[SPEAKER_00]: Maintain that context and you are eroding the ownership.

283
00:23:00,678 --> 00:23:04,140
[SPEAKER_00]: The longer you're maintaining, the less and less ownership there will be remaining.

284
00:23:05,070 --> 00:23:07,231
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not to say that it's going to be pleasant or inspiring.

285
00:23:07,511 --> 00:23:08,071
[SPEAKER_00]: Quite the opposite.

286
00:23:08,111 --> 00:23:08,772
[SPEAKER_01]: But once that...

287
00:23:09,072 --> 00:23:16,595
[SPEAKER_01]: So, once that ownership is gone, so to speak, then that... Dispassion.

288
00:23:16,615 --> 00:23:17,615
[SPEAKER_01]: There's still anicca.

289
00:23:18,616 --> 00:23:29,100
[SPEAKER_00]: But that anxiety there changes... Well, because you're not trying to outrun it, you're not trying to get rid of it, which means it's not going to be unpleasant.

290
00:23:30,361 --> 00:23:32,322
[SPEAKER_00]: It's going to be just a neutral state of things.

291
00:23:33,254 --> 00:23:36,295
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you wouldn't really call it anxiety anymore, from that point of view.

292
00:23:36,715 --> 00:23:42,737
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and you said in the past that you might have called it being alert.

293
00:23:42,877 --> 00:23:43,337
[SPEAKER_00]: Alertness.

294
00:23:43,417 --> 00:23:45,978
[SPEAKER_00]: Recognition that, yeah, things might end next second.

295
00:23:46,078 --> 00:23:47,158
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yeah, they might.

296
00:23:47,178 --> 00:23:49,039
[SPEAKER_00]: The mindfulness of death that the Buddha encouraged.

297
00:23:49,379 --> 00:23:50,299
[SPEAKER_00]: That's exactly that way.

298
00:23:50,319 --> 00:23:53,980
[SPEAKER_00]: First, you'll be anxious, because the assumption of life is so strong.

299
00:23:54,381 --> 00:24:01,423
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you have uprooted the assumption of life, then it's just recognition that, yeah, I literally might die within the next in-and-out breath.

300
00:24:02,468 --> 00:24:06,956
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's just you're alert to that fundamental, very profound recognition.

301
00:24:07,237 --> 00:24:09,802
[SPEAKER_00]: Somehow, from anxiety...

302
00:24:11,878 --> 00:24:19,341
[SPEAKER_01]: When you understand things, when you get the right view, that anxiety changes into alert mindfulness.

303
00:24:19,381 --> 00:24:19,961
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't change.

304
00:24:20,041 --> 00:24:21,621
[SPEAKER_00]: It always was that.

305
00:24:21,641 --> 00:24:22,061
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the thing.

306
00:24:22,202 --> 00:24:27,083
[SPEAKER_00]: Anxiety is already alertness that you're resisting, which is why that alertness is unpleasant.

307
00:24:27,103 --> 00:24:33,485
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why mindfulness, responsibility, recognition of your situation, are all kind of unpleasant recognition for Putujana.

308
00:24:35,306 --> 00:24:38,047
[SPEAKER_00]: So... and that's why Putujana cannot become a Sotāpanna.

309
00:24:39,398 --> 00:24:42,019
[SPEAKER_00]: By chance, by miracle, or by technique.

310
00:24:43,080 --> 00:24:51,924
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the direction of Sotāpati, the right view and freedom, is through the anxiety that entire being of Kutujana is geared against.

311
00:24:52,545 --> 00:24:53,765
[SPEAKER_00]: Always.

312
00:24:53,805 --> 00:25:01,970
[SPEAKER_00]: By default, ownership, sense of self, self-view, all of that is against that which is behind the anxiety.

313
00:25:02,270 --> 00:25:04,851
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why anxiety is so terrifying and unpleasant.

314
00:25:05,591 --> 00:25:08,413
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even when people practice to be free from anxiety,

315
00:25:10,127 --> 00:25:18,531
[SPEAKER_00]: That practice will not result in liberation, because it's taking you away from that which is inherently revealed by anxiety that you cannot bear.

316
00:25:19,291 --> 00:25:21,812
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the practice should not be freeing you from anxiety.

317
00:25:22,253 --> 00:25:25,174
[SPEAKER_00]: It should be making you less disturbed by anxiety.

318
00:25:26,955 --> 00:25:31,857
[SPEAKER_00]: You get, like... Saying being free from anxiety means, oh, being free from existence while you still exist.

319
00:25:32,357 --> 00:25:33,278
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a contradiction in terms.

320
00:25:33,318 --> 00:25:34,698
[SPEAKER_00]: It's impossible.

321
00:25:34,778 --> 00:25:35,879
[SPEAKER_00]: Anxiety is the existence.

322
00:25:37,161 --> 00:25:44,494
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if it's a problem, it's not because of the existence of the anxiety.

323
00:25:45,015 --> 00:25:47,279
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because of your assumptions in regard to it.

324
00:25:49,773 --> 00:25:57,875
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the practice should be enabling you to accept that, and endure it, and then eventually not even being bothered by it, and then see exactly what's there.

325
00:25:58,275 --> 00:25:59,815
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the direction of sotāpati.

326
00:26:00,275 --> 00:26:06,817
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I said before, like, when the suttas say, or at the moment of death, they will achieve the fruit, these various noble disciples.

327
00:26:06,897 --> 00:26:15,299
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because there's some mystical things happening in the moment of death, it's because death is at the end of that direction, that you don't want to look at, when you experience anxiety in day-to-day life.

328
00:26:16,615 --> 00:26:18,602
[SPEAKER_00]: But you will have to go through it, eventually.

329
00:26:19,305 --> 00:26:22,476
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the one with the right view goes that way even before he dies.

330
00:26:25,149 --> 00:26:34,377
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's why the sotāpanna can become an arahant at the moment of death.

331
00:26:34,977 --> 00:26:37,440
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's the direction where his sotāpatti already is.

332
00:26:38,160 --> 00:26:41,463
[SPEAKER_01]: He's facing that nature with no distractions.

333
00:26:41,863 --> 00:26:45,647
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the danger for any distractions for the sotāpanna.

334
00:26:45,707 --> 00:26:47,028
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why he doesn't make any progress.

335
00:26:47,680 --> 00:26:48,660
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the distractions, yeah.

336
00:26:48,680 --> 00:26:52,862
[SPEAKER_00]: They just keep him, basically, slowing him down from heading the way that he's heading.

337
00:26:52,922 --> 00:26:53,102
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

338
00:26:53,502 --> 00:26:59,504
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why, also, all a noble disciple needs to do is not be negligent and make the effort towards distractions.

339
00:26:59,944 --> 00:27:06,686
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if he doesn't make the effort towards distractions, what he's left with is his right view, which means he's left with the practice.

340
00:27:06,706 --> 00:27:10,147
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all he needs to do is abstain from unwholesome things and he'll become an arahant.

341
00:27:10,167 --> 00:27:11,708
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't need to do anything on top of that.

342
00:27:14,597 --> 00:27:14,738
Hmm.

